/-!
# SL₂(ℤ)-invariance plus holomorphic q-expansion forces constancy

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.coeff_eq_zero_of_hasSum_of_slash_invariant`
(`0e938601-a688-501e-85a5-4562c1fd5267`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `0d689232-56a2-596e-9b69-a9106d3230c1`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $F \colon \mathfrak{H} \to \mathbb{C}$ be a function on the upper half-plane and let $(c_m)_{m
\ge 0}$ be a sequence of complex numbers. Two hypotheses are imposed. First, for every $\tau \in
\mathfrak{H}$ the family $m \mapsto c_m\, q^m$, where $q = e^{2\pi i \tau}$ is Mathlib's
$q$-parameter `Function.Periodic.qParam 1` evaluated at $\tau$, is summable with sum $F(\tau)$; thus
$F$ is everywhere given by a convergent power series in $q$ with no polar part and no negative-index
terms. Second, $F$ is invariant under the Möbius action of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ on
$\mathfrak{H}$: $F(\gamma \cdot \tau) = F(\tau)$ for all $\gamma \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ and
all $\tau \in \mathfrak{H}$. The conclusion is that $c_m = 0$ for every index $m \ne 0$.
Consequently $F$ is the constant function with value $c_0$. No holomorphy or growth condition on $F$
is assumed beyond the existence of the convergent $q$-expansion itself.

This is the weight-zero Liouville step: a modular function for the full level-one group which is
holomorphic on $\mathfrak{H}$ and at the cusp is constant, here recorded as the vanishing of all
higher $q$-expansion coefficients. It is used in the proof that the coefficients of the modular
polynomial, viewed as functions on the upper half-plane, lie in the ring generated by $j$, via
[`ModularCurve.mem_adjoin_jq_of_hasSum_of_slash_invariant`]
(thm.html#ModularCurve.mem_adjoin_jq_of_hasSum_of_slash_invariant).

